From fa15c196a48798a0017eda26da12ee965f049ddb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nightshift <noreply@example.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 08:28:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(worker): refuse a green suite that undid the upgrade
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Probing the policy engine rather than reading it turned up five ways for the
repair agent to make the suite green without fixing anything. Three were inert
by accident: the loop decides from its own fixed-argv test run, so --deselect,
--ignore and -k never reach the verdict. That accident is now pinned by a test.

One was real. `pip install jinja2==2.11.3` reinstalls the vulnerable version,
the suite goes green, the job ends PATCHED_REPAIRED and a pull request opens
with the advisory still unfixed. The instruction prompt tells the agent not to
do this, and the whole argument of this project is that an instruction is not a
guarantee.

Two defences, in order of importance:

- After a green suite, the installed version of every upgraded package is read
  out of the sandbox and compared to the fixed version. Drift means the green
  proves nothing: the attempt is recorded as failed and the agent is told why,
  so it does not spend its remaining attempts the same way. This catches every
  route to a false green, including ones nobody has thought of.
- The policy engine gains a no-downgrade rule for the packages a job came to
  upgrade. `pip install -r requirements.txt` stays allowed, because the manifest
  already carries the new pin. Naming the package is what is refused.

Also fixes the wall-clock ceiling, which only accumulated per-attempt durations
and therefore excluded clone, environment build and both full suite runs — the
slowest phases of a job. A repository taking twenty-five minutes to install was
entering the repair loop with a ceiling that had not started counting. Budget
now has one clock, started once and read everywhere.
